207:Each run seed derives separate streams. The stream seed is the first 8 bytes of the SHA-256 of the UTF-8 text `<run seed>|<label>`, read as a big-endian unsigned 64-bit integer. Labels: `tape`, `choice`, `reservoir`, `replay`, `shuffle`, `filler`, `features`. Each stream is `xoshiro256**` with its 256-bit state filled by four SplitMix64 outputs from the stream seed (the standard constructions). Uniform integer in 0 to m−1: draw 64-bit outputs, accept one only if it is below 2^64 − (2^64 mod m), and return it mod m. Uniform real in [0, 1): the top 53 bits divided by 2^53. Normal (random projection only): Box–Muller from two uniform reals, with the first replaced by 2^−53 if it is 0. Permutations: Fisher–Yates driven by the uniform-integer rule. Run seeds are fixed before any run: stage 1 uses the original six seeds 4101–4106 (development data, as the original spec states); stage 2 uses 20261201, 20261202 and 20261203. One stream never feeds another's decisions, so, for example, admitting a lesson cannot change which entry is replayed next except through the diary's contents.
248:All stages use development sets until stage 3 opens the transfer protocol's locked sets, once. Bootstrap: resample whole games with replacement 10,000 times, seed 20261117, and take the 2.5% point of the paired difference as the lower limit, as in the [transfer protocol](../research-goals/transfer-and-combination-protocol-2026-11-03.md) (which uses seed 20261103 for its own gates). Every pass rule must hold in every run or seed.
264:Every comparison arm receives **identical online lessons** to the diary arm (same inputs, same pulses, same order). The five replay controls (no old replays, filler, moved timing, no pulse, event-shuffled) are copies of the diary arm's replay log that change **only the replays of old lessons**, meaning entries written in an earlier block. Replays of the current block's own lessons stay exactly as in the diary arm. So through the first block every replay control is in exactly the diary arm's state, and after that the only difference is what happened to the old-lesson replays, which is what the main hypothesis is about. No state is copied between arms after the first block, so later differences are the accumulated effect of each arm's own replay changes, not an isolated intervention from identical starts. The dry run checks that the first-block snapshot of each replay control equals the diary arm's, bit for bit. Stage 2 does this with a fixed tape. In stage 1 the diary arm makes its own choices, as in the original task, and each added arm is **yoked** to them: it receives the diary arm's chosen input and pulse at every trial instead of choosing. The task's four original arms keep their own choices and serve only to reproduce the original marks. The case-recall arms and the two learners are run in stage 2 as descriptive results and are decisive only in stage 3.
270:| **No old replays** | Identical to the diary arm, except that every old-lesson replay is skipped: no step at all. It matches the diary arm bit for bit through the first block, gets the same lessons and the same current-block replays, and after the first block carries its own state forward (nothing is copied from the diary arm) | The reference for forgetting and for filler damage: it differs from the diary only by the missing old-lesson replays |
272:| **Moved timing** | Copies the diary arm's replay log from the same run. Every replay is kept. For each block (stage 1: each item block and the reversal; stage 2: A and B), the replays made during that block of entries from earlier blocks are moved, in the same order, to the end of the block before it, after that block's test snapshot and before the next block starts; replays of the current block's own entries stay where they were | Rival 2b |
282:**Case recall, exact rule.** Keys, all fixed before data with the `features` stream: (a) raw input vector; (b) Kenyon-cell rates: the rates of the untrained learner in one forward pass on the input, with no learning and no dopamine; (c) random features: a projection z = Wx, where W has one row per Kenyon cell with independent standard normal entries drawn from the `features` stream, row by row. Keep the k units with the largest z (ties go to the lower unit index); the kept units carry max(z, 0) and all others are zero. k is the mean number of active Kenyon cells per development input at the untrained state, rounded half up, computed once from the development inputs before any data. Each key is scaled to unit length; distance is Euclidean. A candidate's score is the inverse-distance-weighted mean of the pulse signs (+1 reward, −1 punishment) of its 3 nearest stored lessons, weight 1 / (d + 10⁻⁹); if any stored lesson is at distance below 10⁻¹², the score is the mean sign over those exact matches only. Ties and tie tolerance follow the fly's scoring. The bar is the best of the three keys in each version, chosen per stage on the final result: this favours the control.
284:**Simple learners, exact rule.** Score = w · φ(x), with w starting at zero. On each lesson or replay with pulse sign s: w ← w + s φ(x) / (‖φ(x)‖² + 10⁻⁹). φ is the raw input (the dense vector of Eyes v2 current on each photoreceptor, zero where the stimulus is absent) or the random features above. There is no learning rate to tune: because w starts at zero and every update would be multiplied by the same rate, a rate only rescales all scores and cannot change any ranking.
337:**Worked example of the unknown-board rule.** Diary arm: 192 eligible development boards, 183 scored (90 good) and 9 unknown. Other arm: 192 scored, 90 good. Worst case for the diary: its 9 unknowns score 0, so 90 of 192 against 90 of 192: difference 0, and no advantage can pass. Left out instead, 90 of 183 (0.49) against 90 of 192 (0.47) would look like an advantage out of nothing; that version is reported beside the main result and cannot pass alone.
349:**Who is trained on what.** Each fly arm chooses for itself, as its protocol specifies, so fly arms see different lessons. Each case-recall arm and each learner with the diary is fed the complete receipt stream of the fly it is compared with, in the same order: the same lessons and pulses, plus that fly's replays, taken from its receipts. The protocol's missing-idea and filler conditions are kept as the protocol defines them, so a control that lacks an idea is never given the omitted lessons. For the central attribution test (the full fly against the case-recall and learner arms), the learner arms are matched to the evaluated full fly's actual lessons and replay tape, so the learner is the only difference. The auditor checks that each matched arm's receipt stream equals its fly's, byte for byte.
351:A **reasoning claim for the fly plus diary** at Phase C step 2 (three ideas) requires, in all three runs, with every audit passed:
364:3. **Prerequisite not met:** the stage's forgetting prerequisite fails: section 7.3 for stage 1, and for stage 2 the no-old-replays arm (which learned A exactly as the diary arm did) must forget during B, or the stage says nothing about the diary. The no-diary arm's result is reported beside it, but it does not decide the prerequisite.
387:5. Order: stage 1 can be built first. Stage 2 needs the twin-pair generators, which do not exist yet, a game manifest showing enough eligible boards for three disjoint practice sets (1,200 training-pool games exist; v8 used 750 positions from 198 games), and worked scoring examples for the boundary cases above. Launch gates for stage 2, checked before its run: G1, the game manifest with eligible-board counts for the three disjoint sets; G2, the twin-pair generators with unit tests; G3, the worked scoring examples committed and checked against the scorer. These gates are not design defects; the stage does not start until each is met. The three-board feedback test and the 2026-11-09 decision remain open and are unchanged.
398:| Peer 4 and skeptic 2: moved-timing arm dropped new-block replays; own-choice arms see different lessons | Moved timing keeps every replay; stage 2 uses one fixed tape; dopamine factors and update sizes logged; feedback-cut descriptive arm |
446:2. **Peer, required to launch: reproducibility.** Stream derivation, generators and run seeds are fixed (section 4.8). Launch gates G1 to G3 are named (section 11).
14:- **Development set:** a separate set used only to choose the saved memory snapshot.
16:- **Bootstrap lower limit:** resample whole games with replacement 10,000 times (seed 20261103) and take the 2.5% point of the difference in correct pairs. Implemented as `pairGate` in [transfer-evaluation-v1.mjs](../../../.codebase/src/neural-learning/transfer-evaluation-v1.mjs).
17:- **Run:** one complete training and test with one puzzle order and one choice seed. Three runs are not three flies.
25:5. **Equal practice:** every arm gets the same number of feedback trials in the same order, with the same seeds. An arm that skips a training block gets the same number of **filler trials** instead: the non-chess arbitrary-pairing trials of the 2026-11-01 pilot, which carry feedback but no chess fact.
119:**Arms:** Full fly (ideas 1 and 2); idea-1-only fly (480 filler trials in place of idea 2); idea-2-only fly (filler in place of idea 1); fresh fly (960 filler trials); shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; and the **fixed-sum combiner**, which adds the idea-1-only fly's and idea-2-only fly's move scores with a fixed rule. The fixed-sum combiner tests the review's warning that a hand-written rule could combine two answers without the fly learning to combine.
123:1. **C1-a:** Full fly at least 44/60.
141:**Arms:** Full fly; each two-idea fly (filler in place of the missing idea); each single-idea fly; fresh fly; shuffled-feedback fly; plasticity-off fly; simple learner; similar-board recall; exact-recall store; fixed-sum combiner of the three single-idea flies.
145:1. **C2-a:** Full fly at least 44/60.
154:3. Training stops at the fixed trial counts. Snapshot choice and early stops follow the Phase B rules.
161:- Full fly no better than its best fewer-idea arm: no combination.
162:- Full fly no better than the fixed-sum combiner: the combination can be explained by a fixed addition of separate scores.
163:- Full fly no better than similar-board recall or the simple learner: input similarity or a simpler learner explains the result.
